  "tool.ptonhelix.title": "Point on helix",
  "@tool.ptonhelix.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Point on helix\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"points\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a coil to add a point riding it, at an angle measured from where the coil starts, the way it turns.",
    "x-translated": "ce415157"
  },
  "tool.ptonhelix.help": "Click a coil to add a point riding it, at an angle measured from where the coil starts, the way it turns. The angle is not modular: 450 deg is the second winding, one pitch above 90 deg, and typing a bigger number walks up the spring. In the plan every winding is drawn on top of the same circle, so a click there can only mean the first winding (0-360 deg) and a drag keeps the winding the point is on; in the 3D view the pointer meets one particular winding, so a click there states the angle straight away and a drag slides the point along the whole coil. Type the angle in the panel to reach any winding from either view. An angle past the end of the coil says so and comes back when you raise the turn count. Left-hand coils count the same way — the angle follows the curve.",
  "@tool.ptonhelix.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Point on helix\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"points\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a coil to add a point riding it, at an angle measured from where the coil starts, the way it turns.",
    "x-translated": "7749490b"
  },
  "tool.ptonhelix.slot.1": "coil",
  "@tool.ptonhelix.slot.1": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Point on helix\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"points\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a coil to add a point riding it, at an angle measured from where the coil starts, the way it turns.",
    "x-translated": "bb67f671"
  },
  "tool.keypoints.title": "Key points",
  "@tool.keypoints.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Key points\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"points\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a curve to add its defining points (endpoints, centre) — or a wall footprint / traced area for its corners, which are then snappable and dimensionable like any point.",
    "x-translated": "79fecc67"
  },
  "tool.keypoints.help": "Click a curve to add its defining points (endpoints, centre) — or a wall footprint / traced area for its corners, which are then snappable and dimensionable like any point. Works on mirrored and derived geometry too. A curve in space gives its start and its end, and a coil its centre as well: those are points in space, drawn where they project in the plan and where they stand in the 3D view, and they follow the curve through every edit.",
  "@tool.keypoints.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Key points\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"points\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a curve to add its defining points (endpoints, centre) — or a wall footprint / traced area for its corners, which are then snappable and dimensionable like any point.",
    "x-translated": "c8fe2750"
  },
  "tool.keypoints.slot.1": "curve or area",
  "@tool.keypoints.slot.1": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Key points\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"points\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a curve to add its defining points (endpoints, centre) — or a wall footprint / traced area for its corners, which are then snappable and dimensionable like any point.",
    "x-translated": "be3ae310"
  },
