  "tool.combineviews.title": "Combine two views",
  "@tool.combineviews.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Combine two views\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"curves\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click the route drawn in one space — the plan — then switch the sketch plane and click the route drawn in the other — the elevation: the curve in space whose shadow in each of them is the curve you drew there.",
    "x-translated": "239a57e1"
  },
  "tool.combineviews.help": "Click the route drawn in one space — the plan — then switch the sketch plane and click the route drawn in the other — the elevation: the curve in space whose shadow in each of them is the curve you drew there. This is how a route was laid out on a drawing board, and both views stay ordinary drawings: drag a point of either, or tilt either space, and the run follows. The two spaces must meet (parallel ones have no common direction), each view must run one way along that common direction, and only the stretch both views cover becomes a run.",
  "@tool.combineviews.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Combine two views\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"curves\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click the route drawn in one space — the plan — then switch the sketch plane and click the route drawn in the other — the elevation: the curve in space whose shadow in each of them is the curve you drew there.",
