  "tool.projectface.help": "Click a curve you have drawn, then a solid: the curve is thrown onto that body's face along the way you are looking at it — straight down out of the plan, or out of whatever sketch plane you drew it on — and what comes back is a curve in space lying in that face. An engraved line, a groove to sweep, a route that has to follow the part. Both stay live: drag a point of the drawing, or stretch the body, and it follows. A segment and a Bézier land exactly; a circle lands as the ellipse it really is. A face standing edge-on to your drawing says so and heals when you tilt the plane, and an imported body has no named faces to land on.",
  "@tool.projectface.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Project onto a face\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"curves\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a curve you have drawn, then a solid: the curve is thrown onto that body's face along the way you are looking at it — straight down out of the plan, or out of whatever sketch plane you drew it on — and what comes back is a curve in space lying in that…",
