  "tool.connectg2.title": "Connect two curves (curvature)",
  "@tool.connectg2.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Connect two curves (curvature)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"curves\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: The same gesture as Connect two curves, matching each run's curvature as well as its direction: the bend does not merely leave along the run, it leaves bending as hard as the run was bending, so a tube along the whole route has no visible break in it at all.",
    "x-translated": "d1f3a8cd"
  },
  "tool.connectg2.help": "The same gesture as Connect two curves, matching each run's curvature as well as its direction: the bend does not merely leave along the run, it leaves bending as hard as the run was bending, so a tube along the whole route has no visible break in it at all. It is three cubic pieces instead of one, and it is exact — nothing is fitted; between two curves of one drawing that is three drawing curves, which an outline takes together. Which continuity a join has is which tool you used, so it is what the file records and never changes by itself.",
  "@tool.connectg2.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Connect two curves (curvature)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"curves\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: The same gesture as Connect two curves, matching each run's curvature as well as its direction: the bend does not merely leave along the run, it leaves bending as hard as the run was bending, so a tube along the whole route has no visible break in it at all.",
