  "tool.loftruled.title": "Loft (ruled)",
  "@tool.loftruled.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Loft (ruled)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"solids\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click the closed sections you have drawn on stations of one run — in any order — then press Enter: what you get is the skin over them, straight from each section to the next, capped at both ends with the end sections themselves.",
    "x-translated": "f2151969"
  },
  "tool.loftruled.help": "Click the closed sections you have drawn on stations of one run — in any order — then press Enter: what you get is the skin over them, straight from each section to the next, capped at both ends with the end sections themselves. Switch station between clicks and the picks are kept. The sections run in the order of their stations' own distances, so sliding a station slides the skin with it and every section stays a live sketch you can reshape. Corresponding curves are paired by going round each outline the same way: with the same number of pieces on both sections that is all it takes. Where the counts differ, either split a curve with *Break* so they agree, or state one pair with *Match sections* — then what nobody matched collapses honestly to a point, which is how a rectangle becomes a triangle. Every strip and both caps are faces of the body: click one in the 3D view to sketch on it, give it its own colour, or cut through it.",
  "@tool.loftruled.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Loft (ruled)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"solids\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click the closed sections you have drawn on stations of one run — in any order — then press Enter: what you get is the skin over them, straight from each section to the next, capped at both ends with the end sections themselves.",
    "x-translated": "194abb6d"
  },
  "tool.loftruled.slot.1": "section",
  "@tool.loftruled.slot.1": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Loft (ruled)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"solids\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click the closed sections you have drawn on stations of one run — in any order — then press Enter: what you get is the skin over them, straight from each section to the next, capped at both ends with the end sections themselves.",
    "x-translated": "2d737aef"
  },
  "tool.loftfair.title": "Loft (faired)",
  "@tool.loftfair.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Loft (faired)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"solids\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: The same gesture as *Loft (ruled)* and the same correspondence, with one difference: instead of running straight from each section to the next, the skin follows one smooth curve through **all** the stations — so a three-station hull swells through its middle…",
    "x-translated": "eec1f87d"
  },
  "tool.loftfair.help": "The same gesture as *Loft (ruled)* and the same correspondence, with one difference: instead of running straight from each section to the next, the skin follows one smooth curve through **all** the stations — so a three-station hull swells through its middle section instead of kinking at it. It still passes exactly through every section you drew. Two sections make the same body either row builds, since a curve through two points is a straight line.",
  "@tool.loftfair.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Loft (faired)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"solids\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: The same gesture as *Loft (ruled)* and the same correspondence, with one difference: instead of running straight from each section to the next, the skin follows one smooth curve through **all** the stations — so a three-station hull swells through its middle…",
    "x-translated": "f3198e7b"
  },
  "tool.loftfair.slot.1": "section",
  "@tool.loftfair.slot.1": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Loft (faired)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"solids\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: The same gesture as *Loft (ruled)* and the same correspondence, with one difference: instead of running straight from each section to the next, the skin follows one smooth curve through **all** the stations — so a three-station hull swells through its middle…",
    "x-translated": "2d737aef"
