  "tool.planeheight.title": "Plane at height",
  "@tool.planeheight.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Plane at height\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Type a height (or pick a parameter in the panel), then click anywhere: a new sketch plane parallel to the one you are in, that far along its normal — no line and no solid to pick.",
    "x-translated": "6b8a0b0b"
  },
  "tool.planeheight.help": "Type a height (or pick a parameter in the panel), then click anywhere: a new sketch plane parallel to the one you are in, that far along its normal — no line and no solid to pick. Everything the plane cuts is drawn there and can be clicked as an input: the section of every solid built before it. Extrude builds along the plane's normal, Cut goes the other way. The height stays a parameter — retype it and the plane slides, with everything drawn on it.",
  "@tool.planeheight.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Plane at height\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Type a height (or pick a parameter in the panel), then click anywhere: a new sketch plane parallel to the one you are in, that far along its normal — no line and no solid to pick.",
    "x-translated": "71952316"
  },
  "tool.sketchplane.title": "Sketch plane (line + angle)",
  "@tool.sketchplane.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Sketch plane (line + angle)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Type an angle (90° if you type none) — and, if you want the plane moved off the line, an offset after it — then click a line, segment or wall leg: the 2D view switches to a new sketch plane through that line, tilted by that angle out of the space you are in…",
    "x-translated": "d19579d3"
  },
  "tool.sketchplane.help": "Type an angle (90° if you type none) — and, if you want the plane moved off the line, an offset after it — then click a line, segment or wall leg: the 2D view switches to a new sketch plane through that line, tilted by that angle out of the space you are in and shifted along its own normal by the offset. So 0° with an offset is a plane *parallel* to the one you are in, which is what a stack of loft sections wants. u runs along the line, v rises out of the old plane; Extrude builds along the new plane's normal and Cut goes the other way, so a negative angle swaps them. Both numbers stay parameters — retype either and the plane moves, with everything drawn on it.",
  "@tool.sketchplane.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Sketch plane (line + angle)\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Type an angle (90° if you type none) — and, if you want the plane moved off the line, an offset after it — then click a line, segment or wall leg: the 2D view switches to a new sketch plane through that line, tilted by that angle out of the space you are in…",
    "x-translated": "9c310a5a"
  },
