  "tool.sketchface.title": "Sketch on face",
  "@tool.sketchface.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Sketch on face\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a straight footprint edge of a solid: the 2D view switches to that face, where that edge lies on the x axis with the origin at its middle and v runs up into the face (Space origin moves it onto a corner).",
    "x-translated": "136defd5"
  },
  "tool.sketchface.help": "Click a straight footprint edge of a solid: the 2D view switches to that face, where that edge lies on the x axis with the origin at its middle and v runs up into the face (Space origin moves it onto a corner). In the 3D view, click the face itself — including a flat end no footprint edge projects to, like the top of a plate. A flat face of a lofted solid works too — every face of a pyramid — and so does a turned part's flat end; a curved one (a barrel, a cone, a ball) says which surface it is and points at Sketch plane. On a partial revolve, clicking inside the profile rather than on an edge takes the cap standing in this plane. Clicking a face that already has a space shows that space instead of making a second one. The part's section at that plane is drawn there, and its edges and corners can be clicked as inputs. Cut there drills into the material; Extrude builds a boss out of it.",
  "@tool.sketchface.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Sketch on face\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Click a straight footprint edge of a solid: the 2D view switches to that face, where that edge lies on the x axis with the origin at its middle and v runs up into the face (Space origin moves it onto a corner).",
    "x-translated": "7e85157"
  },
  "tool.spaceorigin.title": "Space origin",
  "@tool.spaceorigin.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Space origin\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: In a face or datum view: click a corner of any section on this plane — the part's, or any other solid the plane cuts — and the drawing's origin moves there — coordinates are then measured from that corner, and the origin follows it through every edit.",
    "x-translated": "ad5caeb1"
  },
  "tool.spaceorigin.help": "In a face or datum view: click a corner of any section on this plane — the part's, or any other solid the plane cuts — and the drawing's origin moves there — coordinates are then measured from that corner, and the origin follows it through every edit. Type dx (and dy) first to sit a fixed offset away from it; both stay parameters. Anchoring a plane that already carries a sketch moves that sketch with the frame — which is how a whole sketch is shifted on its face. The corner has to be there already: empty space places nothing here, because a point drawn on this plane would move with the frame it was defining.",
  "@tool.spaceorigin.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Space origin\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: In a face or datum view: click a corner of any section on this plane — the part's, or any other solid the plane cuts — and the drawing's origin moves there — coordinates are then measured from that corner, and the origin follows it through every edit.",
    "x-translated": "59c03ada"
  },
  "tool.spaceorigin.slot.1": "anchor corner",
  "@tool.spaceorigin.slot.1": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Space origin\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"planes\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: In a face or datum view: click a corner of any section on this plane — the part's, or any other solid the plane cuts — and the drawing's origin moves there — coordinates are then measured from that corner, and the origin follows it through every edit.",
    "x-translated": "49a3351"
  },
