  "tool.fillet.title": "Fillet",
  "@tool.fillet.title": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Fillet\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"construct\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Type a radius (or pick a parameter in the panel), then click the two legs — lines, segments, circles or arcs — where you want the rounding to touch them.",
    "x-translated": "833e0dd6"
  },
  "tool.fillet.help": "Type a radius (or pick a parameter in the panel), then click the two legs — lines, segments, circles or arcs — where you want the rounding to touch them. The rounding **supersedes the corner**: each leg is trimmed back to the point the arc touches it, so what the drawing shows, traces, extrudes and thickens is the filleted corner and not an arc drawn into a sharp one. The legs keep their names, so an outline, a dimension or a wall built on one follows the trimmed leg; the corner point stays, drawn as construction, and dragging it moves both legs and the rounding with them. On two adjacent legs of an axis-aligned path this *is* that corner's radius — the path's own loop is rounded there, so its extrude, its walls, its key points and its boundary all follow, and the same parameter on two corners keeps them equal. A leg whose tangency falls beyond its own end keeps its full length: the rounding is then tangent to that leg's carrier, which is what it has always been. A radius the corner cannot host says so, names the largest that fits, and comes back the moment the number does.",
  "@tool.fillet.help": {
    "description": "UI text of the \"Fillet\" tool in ConstructIt, a parametric 2D/3D CAD program; toolbar section \"construct\". These strings are the tool's button label, the help sentence shown while it is armed, and the names of the things it asks the user to click. What the tool does: Type a radius (or pick a parameter in the panel), then click the two legs — lines, segments, circles or arcs — where you want the rounding to touch them.",
    "x-translated": "1671fd0a"
  },
  "tool.fillet.slot.1": "leg",
